content:
Keith wrote that it&rsquo;s been really hot and dry, and they&rsquo;ve had almost no breaks from irrigation, but the crops that have been getting water look pretty good. Luckily we have a very high water table under our farm.
They&rsquo;re having problems with a Japanese beetle this summer, though, and haven’t had super great luck stopping them. His girlfriend had the beetles in her rose bushes, and a mix of water and Dawn dish soap killed them. Keith says he uses a lot of Dawn for farm related stuff. &ldquo;It acts like mixing agent that keeps all the chemicals together when spraying and some weeds it helps break down the waxy coating on the outside of the plant helping herbicides penetrate the weeds. The rate I read was actually an ounce per 100gal of total mixture. It’s pretty interesting that it works as good as it does.&rdquo;
Courtney also mentioned the beetles the other day during a phone call; she said they invaded all the trees around the place. (Omg, Dad would have had a cow.) She said that they leave grubs in the lawn, and to control them, you have to kill them at particular times. The almanac says: &ldquo;In the grub stage of late spring and fall (beetles have two life cycles per season), spray the lawn with 2 tablespoons of liquid dishwashing soap diluted in 1 gallon of water per 1,000 square feet. The grubs will surface and the birds will love you. Spray once each week until no more grubs surface.&rdquo; Who knew that dish soap was so useful?
I mentioned that I read that the Asian jumping worm is making its way into Nebraska. He hasn&rsquo;t seen those around, yet. &ldquo;I sure hope we can avoid the worms, it’s starting to feel like every year there is a new insect or weed that’s hard to control. I hope our minimum tillage helps prevent the spread of the worms.&rdquo;
Japanese beetle in the corn:
And a few photos from mid August:

Keith shared some cool videos with us a couple of days ago while he was &ldquo;applying fungicide, insecticide, molasses, and a foliar fertilizer&rdquo; to the soybeans.
Molasses? Yes, &ldquo;it helps feed the microbes in the soil and helps with photosynthesis,&rdquo; Keith says. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve probably ran close to 3 gal. an acre of sugar and molasses products on those. But that molasses also contains a little fertilizer in it.&rdquo;
I text back that he&rsquo;s moving along at a good clip! &ldquo;Somewhere between 16 and 17 mph,&rdquo; he says. &ldquo;My nozzles were just a little too big for the rate I wanted to run. I cover a 120ft swath with that machine. At the rate of speed I was traveling, if I didn&rsquo;t have to turn or fill, I would cover 230 acres an hour or at least that&rsquo;s what the monitor said.&rdquo;
(For a little comic relief, I text some photos of the tomatoes and cucumbers in our back yard. Keith kindly replies that nothing beats fresh vegetables :)
&ldquo;This is something else cool I got to watch the other day on a research plot. I think this is the future of agriculture. It was doing basically the same thing I was doing.&rdquo;
With a tractor, he can carry a HUGE amount of fertilizer, though&hellip; how much liquid can a drone like that could carry? Sure would come in for lots of refills. Maybe the farmer would help with the refills, and have fun overseeing, remote flying, and/or watching? Or&hellip; go out for lunch?
Regardless, seems better than aerial application by a crop duster plane (more precise, less unintentional coverage beyond your field) &ndash; and easier than driving through crops if they get too tall!

summary:
A &ldquo;bomb cyclone&rdquo; caused huge flooding in Nebraska. Our area faired pretty well, but 10 miles north of us was hit hard.
Keith says,
&ldquo;Columbus is a mess from what I&rsquo;ve heard and you can&rsquo;t get to Fullerton very easily&hellip; I wish I could have taken pictures of the Platte River the other day. Dad said he’s never seen the river so full&hellip; We’ve been working on the planter lately. Hopefully it drys up enough we’ll be able to use it. No one around here has any field work done so it will be an awfully busy spring if it does dry out&hellip;

